on flow dynamics.  \n   - **Foundation**: Anchor the structure to the seabed using reinforced concrete or steel piles to withstand pressure and currents.  \n   - **Power Transmission**: Install high-voltage direct current (HVDC) submarine cables to minimize energy loss during transmission to shore. Ensure cables are buried or protected to avoid damage from anchors or marine life.  \n\n3. **Environmental Integration**:  \n   - Incorporate fish-friendly turbine designs to reduce ecological impact.  \n   - Use noise-dampening materials to minimize disruption to marine ecosystems.  \n\n---\n\n**Safety Assessment:**  \n1. **Hazard Identification**:  \n   - **Construction Risks**: Underwater equipment failure, diver safety during installation, and potential leaks from hydraulic systems.  \n   - **Operational Risks**: Turbine malfunctions, cable faults, or biofouling (marine growth on structures).  \n   - **Natural Disasters**: Tsunamis, seismic activity, or extreme weather events.  \n\n2. **Mitigation Strategies**:  \n   - **Structural Resilience**: Design for seismic and flood resistance, using flexible joints to absorb underwater movement.  \n   - **Redundancy**: Include backup power systems and dual-cable networks for critical operations.  \n   - **Monitoring Systems**: Deploy real-time sensors for vibration, pressure, and temperature to detect anomalies.  \n\n3. **Emergency Protocols**:  \n   - Develop plans for rapid turbine shutdown during storms or seismic events.  \n   - Establish spill-response teams and containment systems for potential leaks.  \n   - Conduct regular maintenance and inspections using remotely operated vehicles (ROVs).  \n\n4. **Environmental Safeguards**:  \n   - Perform baseline ecological studies to monitor long-term impacts on marine life.  \n   - Implement artificial reef features to offset habitat disruption.  \n\nThis design prioritizes stability, efficiency, and safety while addressing both technical and environmental challenges. For deta